Subacute Thyroiditis
Endocrine & Metabolic Disorders
Exquisite anterior neck pain/tenderness, Radiation of pain to jaw/ears, Preceding viral illness, Suppressed radioactive iodine uptake

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: EMD-043
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, exquisite pain in the front of the neck, radiating to the jaw, accompanied by a high fever and racing heart.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute onset of severe, unmanageable anterior neck pain, high fever, and significant tachycardia. Immediate out-of-hours assessment was required to exclude acute suppurative (bacterial) thyroiditis, impending thyroid storm, or deep neck space infection before standard clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My neck is so incredibly painful I can barely swallow, the pain shoots right up into my ears, and my heart won't stop racing."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a five-day history of escalating, exquisite focal tenderness and severe pain over the anterior neck. The pain is severely exacerbated by swallowing or turning the head, and frequently radiates to the jaw, ears, and occiput bilaterally. This is accompanied by a high fever, profound fatigue, and severe systemic malaise. The patient reports a recent history of an upper respiratory viral illness three weeks prior to presentation. Over the last 48 hours, they note transient hyperthyroid symptoms, including frequent palpitations, severe heat intolerance, and a fine tremor in the hands.
Functional Status: Confined to bed, highly distressed, holding the neck perfectly rigid to avoid exacerbating the pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of dehydration due to severe pain upon swallowing (odynophagia).

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/80 mmHg | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 38.6 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99 percent on room air | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Visibly unwell, febrile, diaphoretic, and avoiding any movement of the cervical spine.
Physical Exam: Head and neck exam reveals an enlarged, firm, and exquisitely tender thyroid gland on palpation. The extreme tenderness prevents deep palpation of the nodules. There is no overlying skin erythema or fluctuance to suggest an acute abscess. Cervical lymphadenopathy is remarkably absent. Cardiovascular exam confirms sinus tachycardia with a regular rhythm and bounding pulses. Neurological exam notes a fine resting tremor of the outstretched hands. Diagnostic tetrad features observed: Exquisite anterior neck pain/tenderness, Radiation of pain to jaw/ears, Preceding viral illness, and anticipated Suppressed radioactive iodine uptake pending nuclear medicine scans and markedly elevated ESR.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty water glasses present; patient states it hurts too much to drink.
Medication Reconciliation: Takes regular paracetamol and ibuprofen without any significant relief of the severe neck pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Subacute Thyroiditis (De Quervain's Thyroiditis) with thyrotoxicosis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Suppurative Thyroiditis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of overlying skin erythema, fluctuance, or significant cervical lymphadenopathy, and the classic preceding viral prodrome pointing heavily to a granulomatous rather than bacterial process.

Pharyngitis / Tonsillitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The oropharynx is entirely clear on inspection without exudates. The pain is distinctly localized to the thyroid bed externally, radiating upwards, rather than originating in the throat.

Grave's Disease (Thyroid Storm) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The thyroid gland in Grave's disease is typically non-tender and lacks the exquisite, radiating pain pattern seen here, despite the overlapping hyperthyroid symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, highly painful inflammatory condition of the thyroid gland driving a secondary thyrotoxicosis. Triage accurately flagged the exquisite neck pain, high fever, and tachycardia as requiring urgent differentiation from a deep space bacterial infection. Objective evaluation confirmed the classic clinical stigmata of subacute viral thyroiditis. Delaying care until standard business hours forced the patient to endure agonizing pain, risk severe dehydration, and endure unchecked tachycardia. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation was clinically mandated to provide potent anti-inflammatory therapy, initiate acute beta-blockade for the tachycardia, and rule out a surgical neck emergency, directly satisfying urgent MBS criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral propranolol 20mg immediately to provide acute beta-adrenergic blockade, reducing the heart rate and tremor. Prescribed oral prednisone 40mg daily (a high-dose corticosteroid) to rapidly suppress the severe, destructive glandular inflammation, as NSAIDs have already failed. Ordered an urgent morning fasting blood panel including TSH, free T4, free T3, ESR, and CRP.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the prescribed steroid medication strictly with a large meal every morning (action), you buffer the harsh chemicals from directly irritating your stomach lining (cause), directly preventing severe heartburn or stomach ulcers during this intense treatment phase (effect).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Nil.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours to assess the response to steroids, review the thyroid function tests, and arrange a formal ultrasound or radioactive iodine uptake scan if the diagnosis remains uncertain.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops extreme difficulty breathing, a sudden inability to swallow saliva, a sudden spike in fever above 39.5 C, or severe confusion, present to the emergency department immediately or call 000.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe pain and racing heart are classic signs of a severe viral inflammation of the thyroid gland, which is dumping stored thyroid hormones into your blood. Formal blood tests are required to confirm the extent of the inflammation.
Clinical Course: The high-dose steroids usually provide dramatic and rapid relief of the neck pain within 24 to 48 hours. After the initial hyperthyroid phase, you will likely experience a temporary hypothyroid phase (fatigue, cold intolerance) requiring careful monitoring over several months.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Take the beta-blocker exactly as prescribed to protect your heart from the excess hormones. Sip small amounts of ice water continuously to maintain hydration despite the pain.

